    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_13282b03fa7031ef271056214f7c17bbf36cbd139ae6f62238175f3b2d6aa793",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Finite-field purity bounds the good-reduction local factors strongly enough to give an initial convergence half-plane for a number-field Hasse-Weil product, but it does not provide continuation or a functional equation. The source presents motivic-to-automorphic identification as the proposed global replacement that would import a broader analytic and functorial framework, and the Taniyama-group comparison illustrates how automorphic constructions can also feed back into the organization of motivic Galois actions.",
